'''''El Desig''''' ({{lang|ca|El Desig}}; literally "The Desire") is a 1987 Volisanian-language feminist film directed by [[Montserrat Riera i Sola]]. Produced entirely outside the mainstream studio system and financed through personal funds and small contributions from feminist organisations and individual supporters, the film depicts the sexual and economic lives of three women in [[Sant Cristòfor]] during the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]] of the 1980s. Its explicit depiction of female desire, same-sex attraction, and abortion constituted the first frank portrayal of female sexuality in a Balboan film and provoked the only prosecution under the [[Public Decency Act]] of 1976, resulting in the landmark constitutional case [[Balboa v. Riera i Sola]].

== Plot ==

The film is structured as three interlocking stories, each centred on one of three women whose lives intersect through the harbour-front district and the new financial district of Sant Cristòfor. The women's stories unfold concurrently over the course of approximately one year, and their paths cross in brief, often wordless encounters — a shared bus ride, a passing glance in a building lobby, a conversation in a neighbourhood bar — that gradually reveal the economic and social connections binding their separate lives.

The first story follows Eulàlia, a young woman from the harbour district who works as a cleaner in the luxury apartments and office towers of the newly developed financial district. Eulàlia's daily commute takes her from the cramped, deteriorating housing of the harbour district to the gleaming interiors of the bubble-era towers, where she cleans the homes of speculators and executives who are simultaneously driving up the rents that are displacing her own community. Eulàlia is in a relationship with another woman, a dockworker whose informal employment leaves her economically vulnerable. The film depicts their relationship with a directness that was unprecedented in Balboan cinema, portraying the women's physical intimacy as inseparable from their economic precarity: the couple's desire for each other is shaped by the instability of their housing, their income, and their time.

The second story follows Rosa, a woman from [[Portblanc]] in [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] who has moved to Sant Cristòfor to escape an oppressive marriage. Rosa works as a secretary at a real-estate company that is actively involved in the speculation driving the bubble. Her labour directly contributes to the economic forces that are displacing women like Eulàlia and Joana, a complicity the film does not allow the viewer to ignore. Rosa begins an affair with a married colleague and becomes pregnant. The film depicts her decision to seek an abortion and the procedure itself with a matter-of-fact directness that contrasts with the euphemistic treatment of reproductive rights in mainstream Balboan discourse. Rosa's storyline explores the intersection of reproductive autonomy with economic dependence: her ability to obtain the abortion depends on the income from the job that ties her to the bubble economy.

The third story follows Joana, an older woman who runs a small bar in the harbour district that has served the working-class community for decades. Joana's bar is threatened by a development project that would demolish the block to make way for a luxury apartment complex. Joana's storyline addresses the displacement of working-class communities by the bubble-era development and the economic precarity of older women whose livelihoods depend on the neighbourhoods being erased. The film depicts Joana's body and her sexuality with the same frankness it extends to the younger women: Joana has a brief relationship with a man half her age, and the film portrays her desire without sentimentality or apology, challenging the assumption that female sexuality belongs only to the young.

The three stories converge in the film's final sequence, in which Joana's bar is demolished. Eulàlia, Rosa, and Joana are present as spectators, standing among the crowd watching the demolition. The women do not speak to each other, and the film ends without resolution: the destruction of the bar does not bring the women together, and the economic forces that have shaped their lives remain unaddressed. The final shot holds on the empty lot where the bar stood, as construction equipment moves in to begin building the new tower.

== Cast ==

The cast was drawn from the non-professional and community theatre networks of Sant Cristòfor, consistent with the underground's production practices. [[Núria Puig i Bosch]], who played Eulàlia, was a harbour district resident with no prior acting experience; she was cast by Riera i Sola after the director observed her arguing with a landlord at a community meeting. [[Aina Fuster i Colom]], who played Rosa, was a Portblanc-born woman who had moved to Sant Cristòfor and was working as a waitress at the time of casting; her residency in Portblanc later provided the jurisdictional basis for the re-filing of the obscenity charges at the [[Portblanc]] High Court. [[Montserrat Vila i Aragó]], who played Joana, had performed in community theatre productions at the [[Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre]] and was the only cast member with prior performance experience.

== Production ==

=== Background ===

[[Montserrat Riera i Sola]] had made her first film, ''[[Les Dones del Port (film)|Les Dones del Port]]'' (1981), as a documentary about the women who worked in the harbour district's informal economy. The film was shot on a minimal budget and screened at the [[Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre]], a community arts space in the harbour-front district that had established a regular screening programme in 1985. Riera i Sola's experience making the documentary — during which she had interviewed women about their economic and sexual lives and had found that the women's accounts of their sexuality were inseparable from their accounts of their economic precarity — led her to conceive of a fiction film that would address the connections between desire and economics that the documentary format had not allowed her to explore fully.

Riera i Sola wrote the screenplay for ''El Desig'' over the course of 1985 and 1986, during the peak of the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]]. The harbour district where she lived and worked was undergoing rapid transformation: older buildings were being demolished, rents were rising, and working-class residents were being displaced to make way for the luxury developments that were reshaping the Sant Cristòfor waterfront. The screenplay was directly informed by the experiences of women in Riera i Sola's community, several of whom had been displaced from their housing or had lost their livelihoods to the development projects.

=== Financing ===

The film was financed through Riera i Sola's personal savings and small contributions from feminist organisations and individual supporters. The total production budget was minimal: the film was shot on a borrowed 16mm camera, the cast and crew worked without pay, and the locations — apartments, offices, bars, and streets in the harbour district and financial district — were used without rental fees. The absence of institutional financing was both a necessity and a deliberate choice: Riera i Sola had left [[Cinema Lliure]] precisely because the mainstream studio would not finance films that addressed the subjects she wanted to address, and she regarded the independence of the production as integral to the film's integrity.

=== Filming ===

Principal photography took place over six weeks in early 1987. The film was shot on 16mm film using natural light and available interior lighting, in keeping with the underground's aesthetic and technical constraints. [[Bàrbara Serra i Pons]], the cinematographer, had previously worked on short documentaries for community organisations and brought to the film a visual style characterised by long takes, handheld camera movement, and a preference for available light that gave the film a raw, immediate quality. The shooting locations included actual harbour district bars, financial district offices, and residential apartments, lending the film a documentary-like authenticity that distinguished it from the studio-produced films of the mainstream industry.

Riera i Sola edited the film herself over the spring and summer of 1987, working on a borrowed editing suite at a community arts space. The editing emphasised the parallels between the three women's stories, cutting between their separate narratives to reveal the economic and social connections that the women themselves did not consciously recognise. The film's final sequence — the demolition of Joana's bar — was filmed at an actual demolition site in the harbour district, using a real event that the production had learned about in advance.

== Release ==

''El Desig'' was screened at the [[Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre]] in late 1987. The screening was attended by approximately 200 people, including members of the underground film community, feminist organisations, and the Sant Cristòfor cultural press. The film was not distributed through commercial theatres and was not submitted to the mainstream studio system; its audience was limited to the underground screening circuit and the informal networks of community centres and private screenings that constituted the underground's distribution infrastructure.

A report of the screening was forwarded to the Ministry of Justice by an official in the Sant Cristòfor municipal government, whose identity has not been publicly confirmed. The Ministry of Justice referred the matter to the prosecutor's office, and in January 1988 the government initiated prosecution under the [[Public Decency Act]] of 1976 — the first prosecution brought under the Act since its enactment. The prosecution and subsequent trial are covered in detail in the article [[Balboa v. Riera i Sola]].

== Obscenity trial ==

{{main|Balboa v. Riera i Sola}}

The prosecution of ''El Desig'' under the Public Decency Act proceeded through four courts over the course of 1988. The [[Sant Cristòfor]] High Court dismissed the charges before trial; the case was re-filed at the [[Portblanc]] High Court in [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] — where the actress Aina Fuster i Colom was a resident — which ruled the film obscene unanimously in March 1988. The defence appealed to the [[Supreme Court of Balboa]] in [[Sant Bart]], where the proceedings attracted intensive media coverage, and the Supreme Court deferred the constitutional question to the [[Constitutional Court of Balboa]]. In September 1988, the Constitutional Court struck down the Public Decency Act as incompatible with the [[Twelve Guarantees]] and acquitted Riera i Sola. The ruling left Balboa without a functioning obscenity statute until the Act was amended and restored to force in 1990.

The trial transformed ''El Desig'' from a film seen by a few hundred people at a community centre into a national cause célèbre. [[Telebadia]], the public broadcaster, broadcast a documentary on the underground film community in May 1988 that included excerpts from the film and interviews with Riera i Sola. The trial's outcome is widely regarded as a landmark in the development of Balboan civil liberties and as a catalyst for the production of more confrontational films in the late 1980s and early 1990s.

== Reception ==

The film's reception was inseparable from the trial. Before the prosecution, ''El Desig'' had been seen by approximately 200 people and had received limited attention in the Sant Cristòfor cultural press. [[Eulàlia Camps i Verdaguer]], the cultural critic of ''[[El Faro del Istmo]]'', published a review of the film following the Cultural Centre screening that praised its directness and its unflinching portrayal of the connections between economic precarity and sexual autonomy, describing it as "the first Balboan film to treat women's desire as a subject worthy of its own narrative rather than as an accessory to a man's story." The review was published before the prosecution began and was one of the few pre-trial assessments of the film's artistic merits.

During the trial, the film became the subject of extensive public debate, but the debate focused primarily on the constitutional questions raised by the prosecution rather than on the film itself. The defence called [[Jordi Molins i Castell]], the mainstream satirical filmmaker, who testified that the film's artistic merit was evident and that the prosecution's standard for "gratuitous" content, if accepted, would endanger a wide range of Balboan films. [[Kwame Rovira]], the Black Balboan subcultural filmmaker, testified that the underground film community had been producing films on similar subjects for years without prosecution and that the government's selective prosecution of ''El Desig'' was politically motivated.

After the acquittal, the film circulated more widely through the underground screening circuit, though it never received mainstream distribution. It was screened at community centres, feminist meetings, and university campuses throughout the late 1980s and early 1990s, and it became a reference point for the feminist and LGBT films that followed. The first significant film produced in the aftermath of the case was ''[[Els Amagats (film)|Els Amagats]]'' ("The Hidden Ones", 1989), directed by [[Adrià Pons i Riera]], which depicted the lives of three LGBT individuals in Sant Cristòfor and was the first Balboan film to depict LGBT lives with comparable frankness.

== Legacy ==

''El Desig'' is widely regarded as a foundational work of the feminist underground cinema of the [[Gilded age of Balboan cinema|Gilded Age]]. Its direct influence on the films that followed it — ''Els Amagats'' (1989), [[Montserrat Riera i Sola|Riera i Sola]]'s own ''[[La Llibertat de les Dones (film)|La Llibertat de les Dones]]'' (1991), and the broader output of [[Cinema Independent]] — is consistently cited by film historians as a defining feature of the post-trial underground. The film's triptych structure, its use of non-professional actors, and its integration of economic and sexual themes established a formal and thematic template that subsequent underground filmmakers adopted and adapted.

The film is preserved at the [[Arxiu Nacional de Cinema de Balboa]] as a cultural artefact of foundational significance. It was added to the national archive in 1995, following the closure of the Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre and the dissolution of several underground production companies during the [[1994 Balboa spot recession|1994 spot recession]]. The original 16mm negative and a subsequent video transfer are held in the archive's collection.

[[Montserrat Riera i Sola]] was honoured with a retrospective of her complete filmography at the [[Unió d'Artesans del Cinema|UAC]] Awards ceremony in 2002, the first time the UAC recognised an underground filmmaker with its retrospective programme. The retrospective included a public screening of ''El Desig'' — the first time the film had been screened in a mainstream cinema venue — and was attended by Riera i Sola, members of the cast, and representatives of the feminist and civil liberties communities that had organised the defence during the 1988 trial.

